Yes, you can achieve this by implementing a custom validation rule using the JIRA ScriptRunner plugin. The validation rule will check whether all items on the checklist are marked as done before allowing the issue to move to the next column. Here's an example script:

```groovy
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.actions.ValidationResult
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.actions.ValidationRule
import com.onresolve.plugins.scriptrunner.services.ProjectService

class ChecklistValidationRule implements ValidationRule {
    @Field('Checklist')
    String checklistName

    @Override
    def validate(transitions.Transition transition, Map<String, Object> inputs) {
        def project = ProjectService.instance().getProjectObj()
        def issue = transition.getIssue()
        def checklists = issue.getCustomFields().findAll { cf -> cf.getId() == AUI_CHECKLIST }.collect { (cf, values) -> values[0] }
        def checklist = checklists.find { cl -> cl.getName() == checklistName }
        if (!checklist?.all { item -> item.status == 'done' }) {
            return new ValidationResult("Please mark all items on the ${checklistName} checklist as done.")
        }
        return null
    }
}
```

To use this validation rule, follow these steps:

1. Install the JIRA ScriptRunner plugin.
2. Create a new validation rule of type "ChecklistValidationRule" and specify the name of the checklist that should be checked.
3. Associate the validation rule with the transition between the columns you want to restrict.
4. Test the validation rule to ensure it works as expected.

This validation rule will prevent the issue from being moved to the next column if any items on the specified checklist are not marked as done. This helps ensure that all necessary tasks are completed before moving the issue forward in the workflow.

