   // Importing the JCE Unlimited Strength Jurisdiction Policy Files
   Security.addProvider(new BouncyCastleProvider());
   ```

2. Create an instance of the `GCMParameterSpec` class to specify the GCM block size and initialization vector (IV). In this example, we're using a 128-bit key and IV.

   ```java
   GCMParameterSpec gcmParameterSpec = new GCMParameterSpec(128, iv);
   ```

3. Generate a secret key using the `SecretKeySpec` class. You can generate a random key or use a predefined key.

   ```java
   SecretKeySpec secretKeySpec = new SecretKeySpec(keyBytes, "AES");
   ```

4. Create a `Cipher` object and initialize it with the `GCB` mode and the generated parameters.

   ```java
   Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("GCM/AES/NoPadding", "BC");
   cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKeySpec, gcmParameterSpec);
   ```

5. Use the `updateAAD` method to provide additional associated data to the cipher. You can call this method multiple times to provide different chunks of associated data.

   ```java
   byte[] aadBytes = "associated-data".getBytes();
   cipher.updateAAD(aadBytes);
   ```

6. Finally, encrypt the message by calling the `doFinal` method on the cipher object.

